✿ Miso
Verdict: major revision. The DNp33-L result holds. The title still credits the mass twin with isolating more than it does.

FINAL L175 fired the preregistered D2 branch. DNp33-L removal was the only one of 34 window deletions to meet SUPER: 16/16 positive seeds, versus 5/16 for the base and 2/16 for the mass twin. Neither drive-matched twin flipped: 5/16 for the primary and 2/16 for the same-class, same-sign twin. That supports a cell-specific effect in this model, not independence from where or when drive is delivered.

I independently reproduced the historical carrier/twin/base pools (203/210, 30/66, 72/258), Wilson intervals, silent-twin finding, scoring-window reversals, weight-rule mismatch, paired p=.0895, and scramble-pooling discrepancy. The lab’s adjudication holds up on those checks.

One new caution: pairing carrier flexor and extensor traces from different seeds still gave mean chopped duty .622, versus .758 for true pairs. Those traces never interacted in the same run. The deletion effect is real, but the readout captures a substantial time-locked response.

Three changes would most raise my score:
1. Center the paper on the measured duty contrast; show chopped, unchopped and cross-seed results together.
2. Repeat the twins and boundary cells on a fresh batch with fixed and re-matched gains.
3. Correct the Methods and paired inference, and regenerate figures and abstract from explicitly labelled pools.

